{"max_tokens":4096,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"{"claim":"TEAM-AD establishes that vitamin E prevents Alzheimer disease in cognitively healthy adults.","id":"q-5c8ebf600bd85d3a","packet":{"evidence":"TEAM-AD was a randomized placebo-controlled trial at 14 Veterans Affairs centers in 613 patients with established mild-to-moderate Alzheimer disease already receiving an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor. The arms were alpha tocopherol 2,000 IU daily, memantine 20 mg daily, their combination, and placebo. Over mean follow-up 2.27 years, the alpha-tocopherol group had 3.15 points less decline on the ADCS-ADL daily-function scale than placebo (95% CI 0.92 to 5.39; adjusted P=0.03). Memantine and the combination did not show this benefit. An online correction updated one table value and figure/supplement values; this packet uses the indexed corrected abstract and does not use those table/figure values.","source":{"doi":"10.1001/jama.2013.282834","title":"Effect of vitamin E and memantine on functional decline in Alzheimer disease: the TEAM-AD VA cooperative randomized trial.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24381967/","version":"Indexed primary abstract body sha256:121d6786ce30e9584c6f86570e767a23fc1c5f0c08a43c46b1ee927c32286bd6; publication 2014-01-01; online corrected version, correction 10.1001/jama.2014.641"}}}"}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","system":"Classify the claim using only the supplied evidence packet and the common label definitions. Select one permitted label. Report confidence as a number from 0 to 1 representing the probability that your selected label is correct, and provide the concise explanation required by the common output schema. Return only the required JSON object.

Common task dictionary:
Choose one label for the supplied claim relative to the supplied evidence packet.
solid: Claims accurately report the evidence with appropriate scope and caveats. Weak, observational, preclinical or mixed evidence can be reported accurately.
overclaim: The conclusion exceeds the evidence. This is the residual category for inflated framing, unsupported positive or negative generalization, extrapolation to unstudied populations, and surrogate endpoints presented as clinical outcomes.
correlation_vs_causation: A causal claim is drawn from a non-causal, uncontrolled or confounded design; correlation is treated as causation without the needed counterfactual/comparison.
mechanism_vs_evidence: A described biological mechanism (an animal or preclinical pathway) is used to imply human clinical benefit not demonstrated by the evidence. This includes animal-to-human therapeutic and preclinical-pathway-to-therapeutic leaps. A biomarker/surrogate-to-clinical gap alone is excluded from this category.
Precedence: A specific label (correlation_vs_causation or mechanism_vs_evidence) takes precedence over generic overclaim only when its defining trigger is present and captures the core error. Do not stretch a specific definition; otherwise use overclaim. If the claim is appropriately bounded by the evidence, use solid.

